# vendor/ — PACBench-owned isolated Hermes (W1)

**Upgraded Pizza Day validation is enabled; the new Pizza Week remains held**
until native provider and Honcho scheduling checks pass. The isolated Hermes
v0.23.1 integration has
passed a free synthetic local gateway/CLI canary and a separate direct HTTPS
check. Each turn owns and gracefully stops its native gateway. The current
Honcho `honcho-wall-clock-v5` protocol uses a shared-offset Python clock for
Hermes/API/deriver and the reproducible preload library for PostgreSQL only;
OpenSSL and monotonic timers retain real time. Gateway and CLI adoption is bound
to each process identity and turn nonce. The bounded natural startup interval
and native submission/admission guards are documented in the provider map;
CLI launch time is never reported as actual question dispatch. Long homes use
Hermes's authenticated native short-socket pointer, not a guessed socket path. The native system date line supplies a
date, not exact current time. Historical clock receipts retain their original
protocols and cannot resume as v5. These checks establish transport behavior,
not model quality or native provider processing. Keep the persistent OpenRouter
task ledger and its existing uncertain holds; never initialize a replacement.
Every arm must pass native preflight and processing barriers before recall.
The unrelated Muse route
remains blocked if its historical ledger is missing.

This release supports Python 3.14 for normal runtime use. Its lock explicitly
overrides `browser-harness==0.1.13`'s `pillow==12.3.0` requirement with
`pillow==13.0.0`; Hermes PM recognizes this intentional metadata mismatch.
Use the upstream locked/PM installation, not an ordinary unconstrained pip
install. The documented source developer activation selects the `all` extra:

  export HERMES_HOME="$(mktemp -d)"
  export HERMES_RUNTIME_DIR="$HERMES_HOME/tools"
  cd vendor/hermes-isolated
  source ./activate
  hermes pm doctor

Run this in an isolated shell from the PACBench root. Upstream documents this
as a runtime-only developer setup, not the full user-facing installer. PM
activation itself has not been verified here; native provider recall also
remains unverified.

The isolated target environment was built from the upstream lock using the
`all` extra, then the selected client requirements and Memcal were installed.
Reproduce this non-PM-managed environment from the PACBench root, with Python
3.14.9 available and `/path/to/memcal` set to the intended checkout:

  export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT="$PWD/vendor/venv-hermes-isolated"
  (cd vendor/hermes-isolated && uv sync --locked --no-default-groups --extra all --python /path/to/python3.14)
  uv pip install --python "$UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT/bin/python" -r vendor/hermes-isolated-extra-requirements.txt -e /path/to/memcal

The current stable-provider cohort uses Memcal 0.8.11 from an isolated checkout.
Set `PACBENCH_MEMCAL_SRC` to that clean release checkout when its location differs
from the default sibling `memcal-stable-20261113`; source verification requires
commit `e67b8d912484b5b4317dafcff4d60fc975194d8e`. Original development
installations do not determine current runtime versions. Install the additional
client requirements after syncing Hermes and verify the actual distributions.
Hermes v0.23.1 is still the current stable release. Its upstream Pillow lock
override is retained. Offline imports and tests alone do not qualify recall.

- `HERMES_PIN`: pinned upstream remote + full commit SHA (tracked).
- `hermes-isolated/`: git clone of https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent.git
  at that SHA plus exactly `patches/hermes-isolated.diff`; integrity tests reject other edits.
- `venv-hermes-isolated/`: isolated venv, editable install of that clone
  (gitignored). Use with a scratch home, never `~/.hermes`:
  `HERMES_HOME=$(mktemp -d) vendor/venv-hermes-isolated/bin/hermes --version`

The client versions in `hermes-isolated-extra-requirements.txt` document the
target environment; they do not yet establish a PM-managed provisioning
workflow. Install Memcal with its declared dependencies, including pytypedstream,
when provisioning its provider; a source-only import is insufficient.
Run native servers in their own environments: OpenViking requires MCP <2 whereas
Hermes uses MCP 2. Keep Hindsight's API dependencies separate from Hermes' pinned
clients too; its older API releases required an incompatible cryptography version.
The Hindsight SDK stays in Hermes; provisioning starts/stops its real self-hosted
daemon using `vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python`:

  uv venv --python 3.12 vendor/venv-hindsight
  uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
  uv pip install --python vendor/venv-hindsight/bin/python hindsight-all==1.2.1

Use the official CPU PyTorch wheel on Linux CPU hosts; the default GPU wheel pulls
several GB of unused CUDA dependencies. These are hardware/transport installations,
not changes to provider extraction prompts or processing defaults.
Hindsight's server version is separate from Hermes' pinned client version:
the older 0.6.1 API does not implement the native Meta model adapter.

Honcho uses the upstream manual self-hosting checkout at commit
`70c67624169683b9e265517aec95a51b8077cabc` (3.6.1), installed with
`uv sync --frozen --no-dev` in `vendor/honcho-local`. Keep its native batching
and output defaults. Hermes pins the published `honcho-ai==2.9.0` client. A release update does not establish successful ingestion;
verify a small native import and Hermes recall before larger runs.

## Hermes catalog memory providers

The pinned Hermes release moves these providers out of its source tree. Its reviewed
catalog entries identify the following plugin sources and commits (these pins describe
plugin code, not the independently provisioned native servers):

| Provider | Source path | Catalog commit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mem0 | `mem0ai/mem0/integrations/hermes-plugin-mem0` | `43849c6e9d4d773b83a8144b6373aedc67241a5d` |
| Honcho | `plastic-labs/honcho/hermes-plugin-honcho` | `894b88321872e01702a1aa638ddfdde6ad60f173` |
| Hindsight | `vectorize-io/hindsight/hindsight-integrations/hermes` | `f14cc1eb5624b0374cb4d82768eb2e0b9c21ae24` |
| Supermemory | `supermemoryai/hermes-supermemory` | `91405dd00fdf2a0b3515b25c25557598f6793299` |
| OpenViking | `volcengine/OpenViking/examples/hermes-plugin` | `e855796c83f9eb575d9b0f3082926660730f6a63` |

For fresh homes the stable-provider cohort uses Hermes's documented source form
with explicit `--ref --no-deps --no-enable`. `pacbench/catalog_plugins.py`
records selected upstream source revisions separately from the historical
Hermes catalog above. Plugins come from Mem0 2.2.1, Honcho 3.6.1, Hindsight 1.2.1
and OpenViking 0.5.4.1 releases. Supermemory has no standalone plugin tags or
releases; its current declared plugin 2.0.0 is pinned to full revision
`a8f360166419958ce77b876c69f7be3bf0716d67`, requiring SDK 5.0.1.
The published local server remains server-v0.0.9. Native v5 document import,
namespace reads, processing and dreaming completion must all be verified.

Cognee 1.6.8 runs in a private server environment with the unchanged 1.4.3 native
HTTP integration. Its automatic local installer still selects 1.6.6; PACBench
manually provisions the newer local server and records that distinction.
ReMe uses stable v0.4.2.2, including its `core` extra, rather than development
0.4.2.3. Install its shipped Hermes integration from the same release. Preserve
its default resource watcher, extraction, indexing and result-hook witnesses.

MemOS's native local plugin is a separate release from the root Python package.
Install official npm `@memtensor/memos-local-plugin@2.0.21` under
`vendor/memos-local-plugin/`, preserving the verified `release.tgz` and extracted
`package/`. `MEMOS_LOCAL_PIN.json` binds the archive; the harness compares every
shipped file before loading it. Install dependencies with Node's inherited proxy
support (`NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`). Production installation used `--omit=dev
--omit=optional --legacy-peer-deps` because unused DeepSeek Harness development
peers conflict upstream. No runtime source files are rewritten. The native
Hermes bridge, public core bootstrap and raw-document HTTP API remain unchanged.

Each new cohort needs fresh native processing, context injection and tool checks.
Fresh homes disable lazy installs after verified plugin installation. Service
readiness and dependent-write processing receipts remain required; version
updates never imply successful ingestion or recall.
